Body:
If the goal is hardening `auth.json` there may be a better way to do it here; I investigated a narrower atomic-replacement approach related to this issue. Instead of relying on an `lstat` check before writing, `FSUtil.writeJson` can write complete JSON to an exclusive, same-directory temporary file with the requested mode and then rename it over the destination.

Something like:

packages/core/src/fs-util.ts
```ts
const writeJson = Effect.fn("FileSystem.writeJson")(function* (
  path: string,
  data: unknown,
  mode?: number,
) {
  const content = JSON.stringify(data, null, 2);
  const replacementMode =
    mode ??
    (yield* fs.stat(path).pipe(
      Effect.map((info) => info.mode),
      Effect.catch(() => Effect.succeed(undefined)),
    ));
  const temporary = join(dirname(path), `.${crypto.randomUUID()}.tmp`);
  yield* fs
    .writeFileString(temporary, content, { flag: "wx", mode: replacementMode })
    .pipe(
      Effect.andThen(
        replacementMode === undefined
          ? Effect.void
          : fs.chmod(temporary, replacementMode),
      ),
      Effect.andThen(fs.rename(temporary, path).pipe(Effect.uninterruptible)),
      Effect.ensuring(
        fs.remove(temporary, { force: true }).pipe(Effect.ignore),
      ),
    );
});

```

This would avoid following a final-component symlink or hard link during the write, ensure credential files are private from creation, and preserve the previous JSON if writing fails or is interrupted. It would not address cross-process read-modify-write races from #30396 or malformed-file recovery from #29628.

Would maintainers prefer this to replace the `lstat` guard proposed in #36360, build on top of it, or be tracked as a separate issue? If a separate issue is appropriate, I’m happy to open it and work on the patch once assigned.
